--TEST--
`Core\Http\Part::file` refuses a tainted path where `Core\IO` refuses one, while the octets a part carries and the name it is given admit what a user sent — `rule:security/sink-predicate` and `rule:http-server/an-outbound-request-carries-one-body`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A part's `$path` is a path, so it is the sink every path in `Core\IO` is: `..`
// and the separators direct the resolver, and a name a user chose deciding which
// file leaves this process is the whole of that reading
// (`rule:security/sink-predicate`).
tainted string $chosen = "note.txt";

// The refusal, at the one parameter that names a file.
var $refused = Core\Http\Part::file($chosen);

// And the acceptance on the other side of the same bound: a body position
// admits `tainted`, because posting what a user sent is ordinary and a request
// body is not a sink `rule:security/sink-predicate` classifies. So the octets
// and the name a part is given take the same value the path above refused.
var $carried = Core\Http\Part::bytes($chosen, $chosen);
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
  --> case.nvs:9:37
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
